-- Q3612: Security and Control Frameworks [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Aligning controls across frameworks so one implementation satisfies multiple requirements","Eliminating all overlapping regulatory requirements from the compliance program","Replacing multiple frameworks with a single proprietary compliance standard","Ignoring duplicate compliance obligations to reduce administrative overhead"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per the NIST Cybersecurity Framework and COBIT 2019 mapping guidance, control mapping identifies where a single control satisfies overlapping requirements across multiple frameworks. This reduces redundant implementations and streamlines audit evidence collection.
Wrong (B): Control mapping does not eliminate regulatory requirements. Each applicable regulation must still be satisfied; the process simply avoids duplicating controls that serve multiple mandates.
Wrong (C): Control mapping works across existing frameworks rather than replacing them with a proprietary standard. The goal is alignment, not consolidation into one system.
Wrong (D): Ignoring compliance obligations creates regulatory risk and potential penalties. Control mapping addresses overlap through structured alignment, not avoidance.$EXPL$
