-- Q3630: Threats, Attacks, and Vulnerabilities [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Low-severity vulnerabilities never require remediation or patching attention","The penetration test methodology was fundamentally flawed and unreliable","Only vulnerabilities rated critical individually matter for risk assessment","Vulnerability chaining can escalate combined impact beyond individual risk ratings"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-115 Section 7.1 and the OWASP Testing Guide, vulnerability chaining demonstrates that individually low-severity weaknesses can combine into critical exploitation paths. This illustrates why penetration testing adds value beyond automated scanning, which evaluates each finding in isolation.
Wrong (A): Low-severity vulnerabilities may need remediation precisely because they can be chained together. Dismissing them ignores compound risk that individual CVSS scores do not capture.
Wrong (B): The test correctly identified a real attack path by chaining vulnerabilities. Discovering compound risks is a strength of penetration testing methodology, not a flaw.
Wrong (C): Focusing only on critical individual ratings ignores how low-severity findings interact. Compound exploitation paths often represent greater real-world risk than isolated critical findings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3630;

